<article class="disney"><header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Walt Disney World</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Disney</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">−44% exit rate</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Research-Led UX &amp; Information Architecture</p><h1>Public Affairs<br /><span class="heading-accent">Digital</span><br />Transformation</h1></div><p>As Senior UX Strategist through Voce Communications and Porter Novelli, I led research, information architecture, wireframes, and visual design for journalists, community organizations, and people interested in Disney's local impact.</p></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Discovery"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Information architecture"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Delivery"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} /><figure class="hero"><img src="/images/disney_hero.webp" alt="Cinderella Castle at Walt Disney World" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>
<section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-brief"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">The brief</p><h2 id="disney-brief">Prioritize the visitors who needed support.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>Donation seekers represented 43% of engaged traffic in the initial analysis of Disney's Public Affairs site, yet their request path was buried.</p><p>A reported 77% category-page bounce rate added evidence of discovery problems, while journalists faced releases, photos, and videos without clear content-type browsing.</p></div></section>
<section class="research" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p>01 / Discovery</p><h2>Three journeys.<br />One hidden service.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>I audited twelve Fortune 500 Public Affairs sites and three Orlando theme park properties, then reviewed ninety days of heatmaps to locate missed navigation.</p><p>Eight moderated participants across the three audiences revealed orientation problems: some confused Public Affairs with the resort site; others missed navigation or section changes.</p></div><div class="research__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney01.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs desktop design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney02.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs mobile design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney03-1.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs wireframe exploration" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="architecture" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><div class="architecture__head"><p>02 / Information architecture</p><h2>Make the task<br /><span class="heading-accent">the entry point.</span></h2></div><div class="copy"><p>I separated press materials, community support, and impact stories into task-based journeys, giving static information and frequently published material distinct structures.</p><p>Consistent taxonomy resolved overlapping Stories, News, and Releases labels, while modular homepage sections accommodated changing priorities.</p></div><figure><img src="/images/disney04.webp" alt="Disney information architecture wireframes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></section>
<section class="implementation" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p>03 / Responsive delivery</p><h2>A Shorter Flow.<br />A Clearer Interface.</h2></div><div class="implementation__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney05-1.webp" alt="Disney mobile navigation design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney07.webp" alt="Disney responsive UI system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div><div class="copy"><p>The donation path went from more than five interactions to two. All five returning participants completed the task on their first follow-up attempt, compared with two successes among eight participants initially. The smaller returning group provides encouraging evidence, not a population-wide success rate.</p><p>Disney Parks' guidelines informed a distinct Public Affairs identity, with donation requests and community stories prioritized on mobile.</p></div></section>
<section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-ownership"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">Scope &amp; collaboration</p><h2 id="disney-ownership">Carry the research decisions into delivery.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>I supplied specifications and reviewed builds; the development team implemented the front end.</p></div></section>
<section class="results" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><div><p>04 / Results</p><h2>Results &amp;<br />Impact Metrics</h2></div><div class="numbers"><div><b>44%</b><span>decrease in site-wide exit rate</span></div><div><b>24%</b><span>decrease in category-page bounce rate</span></div><div><b>26%</b><span>increase in session duration</span></div></div><div class="copy"><p>The original project account reports post-launch decreases of 44% in site-wide exit rate and 24% in category-page bounce rate, alongside 26% longer sessions. These site-level outcomes do not isolate individual design effects; task observations and analytics each offer an incomplete view.</p></div></section><section class="disney-detail" aria-labelledby="disney-reflection"><div><p class="disney-detail__label">Reflection</p><h2 id="disney-reflection">Observe community partners using the mobile site.</h2></div><div class="disney-detail__copy"><p>I would observe community partners finding support and beginning requests on their own phones to test mobile priorities previously inferred from analytics.</p><p>Fieldwork could reveal interruptions, terminology questions, and practical constraints missed by moderated sessions.</p></div></section></article>
